# Jetpack Compose

## General

Use modern Jetpack Compose and Material 3 conventions.

Prefer:

- Stateless composables where practical.
- State hoisting.
- Unidirectional data flow.
- Small composables.
- Previewable UI where useful.
- Stable UI models.

Avoid putting business logic in composables.

## State

UI state should explicitly represent:

- Loading.
- Content.
- Empty.
- Error.

Avoid multiple unrelated mutable states when a single screen state model is clearer.

Do not use mutable global state.

## Forms

Expense forms should prioritize speed:

- Concept.
- Amount.
- Payer.
- Date.

Use sensible defaults.

Validate input before submission.

Error messages must explain how the user can fix the problem.

## Navigation

Navigation should represent user flows rather than internal implementation details.

Do not pass large domain objects through navigation arguments.

Prefer stable IDs and load the required state in the destination.

## Material 3

Use Material 3 components consistently.

Do not create custom UI components when an appropriate Material component already exists.

Use design tokens/theme values rather than scattering magic dimensions and colors.

## Accessibility

Every interactive element must have an understandable semantic label.

Do not rely only on color to communicate:

- positive balance,
- negative balance,
- errors,
- selected states.

Support:

- TalkBack.
- Font scaling.
- Touch target sizes.
- Sufficient contrast.
- Logical focus/navigation order.

Icons that are purely decorative should not create redundant accessibility announcements.

## UX

The most common action — adding an expense — should be fast.

Avoid unnecessary confirmation dialogs.

Destructive operations such as deleting an expense should provide an appropriate confirmation or undo mechanism.

Use clear financial language:

- "Pagó"
- "Le corresponde"
- "Debe pagar"
- "Debe recibir"
- "Saldado"

Avoid technical terminology in user-facing messages.
